    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points",
    "problem": "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(-5,1,3)$, $Q(-3,-1,2)$, and $R(-5,2,4).$",
    "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨-5,1,3⟩,\\,q=⟨-3,-1,2⟩,\\,r=⟨-5,2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038738/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,4⟩-⟨-5,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={135}^{∘}.\n$$",
